<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ran Wei</h1><p class="st-meta">350 CE – 352 CE · capital Ye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The brief empire of Ran Min, a Chinese general who seized the Later Zhao capital in 350 and ordered the massacre of the Jie and other northern peoples.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a> was a Chinese general adopted into the family of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-hu">Shi Hu</a>. In the civil war after Shi Hu's death he seized Ye, and in 350 he proclaimed himself emperor of a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a> ordered the killing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jie-people">Jie</a> and other non-Chinese in Ye; sources claim some 200,000 died. The massacre made his regime a rallying point for Chinese hostility to the northern conquerors but left it surrounded by enemies. Ran Wei held little more than the Ye region and was constantly at war with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a> remnants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 352 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a> at Liantai; he was executed, and Ye fell later that year. In modern times Ran Min has been both condemned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> and praised by nationalists.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ran-wei.jpg" alt="Ran Wei" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_350_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 CE – 352 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ran Min</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wei (Ran Min)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=351&amp;focus=ran-wei" aria-label="Ran Wei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7559 869 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ran Wei lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7879 1125L7879 1125M7879 1125L7867 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7879 1125h0M7867 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7879 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7879 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ye. <a href="/atlas?year=351&amp;focus=ran-wei">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
